[THOUGHT]  
The diagnosis of Pyoderma Gangrenosum (ulcerative variant) is strongly supported by:  
- Classic clinical morphology (painful, progressive ulcer with violaceous, undermined borders and central necrosis);  
- Definitive pathergy trigger (ant bite at exact site of onset);  
- Major comorbidity (Crohn’s disease — present in ~1–5% of PG cases and highly specific when co-occurring);  
- Comprehensive negative workup excluding infection (bacterial/fungal/mycobacterial cultures + special stains), malignancy, vasculitis, and granulomatous disease;  
- Histopathology showing sterile neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis, organisms, granulomas, or atypia — the hallmark of PG;  
- Absence of red flags for mimics: no systemic symptoms, no lymphadenopathy, no satellite lesions, no travel/infectious exposures, no vascular insufficiency signs (e.g., distal pulses intact, no gangrene, no livedo), and no history of immunosuppression beyond stable Crohn’s therapy.  

All eight domains are fully addressed and internally consistent. The case meets both the *clinical diagnostic criteria* (e.g., ICD-10-CM coding standards, Delphi consensus criteria for PG) and *histopathologic confirmation*. Critically, the diagnosis is not merely “consistent with” PG — it is *diagnostically established*, as PG remains a diagnosis of exclusion *only when key mimics are ruled out*, which has been rigorously accomplished here.  

No critical information is missing. The maximum turn limit (16/16) has been reached, and all necessary data for definitive diagnosis are present.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

